    "answer": {
      "short": "Lines a and f; lines d and e.\n\nEach pair has gradients whose product is $-1$.",
      "worked": "Write each equation in gradient–intercept form:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{a:}\\quad y&=5x-1\\\\\\text{b:}\\quad y&=-\\frac25x+\\frac75\\\\\\text{c:}\\quad y&=\\frac25x+\\frac85\\\\\\text{d:}\\quad y&=\\frac12x+\\frac52\\\\\\text{e:}\\quad y&=-2x+5\\\\\\text{f:}\\quad y&=-\\frac15x-\\frac25\\end{align*}$$\nThe gradients are $5,-\\frac25,\\frac25,\\frac12,-2,-\\frac15$, respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}m_a m_f&=5\\left(-\\frac15\\right)\\\\m_a m_f&=-1\\\\m_d m_e&=\\frac12(-2)\\\\m_d m_e&=-1\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore a and f are perpendicular, and d and e are perpendicular. No other pair has gradient product $-1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
